\chapter{Bionic Actuation and Safety Interlock}

\section{Introduction}

The BAAC-SE v1.0 architecture, authored by independent systems architect
C.~Matthew Blackwell, establishes a cryptographically verified cyber-physical
pipeline in which multi-messenger astrophysical telemetry is transformed into
real-time bionic actuation commands. This chapter formalizes the actuation
model, torque tensor mapping, latency constraints, and the cryptographic
fail-safe mechanisms that guarantee user safety under all operating conditions.

The bionic actuation subsystem is responsible for converting verified
homomorphic computations into physical motion across prosthetic limbs,
exoskeleton frameworks, and neural-interface-driven biomechatronic devices.
Because these systems interact directly with human physiology, the actuation
pipeline must satisfy strict correctness, bounded-error guarantees, and
millisecond-level responsiveness.

\section{Actuation Command Structure}

Each actuation epoch is represented by a structured command vector:
\[
\bm{a}(t) = \{ \tau_1(t), \tau_2(t), \dots, \tau_m(t) \},
\]
where $\tau_i(t)$ denotes the torque applied to joint or actuator $i$ at time
$t$. These torque tensors are derived from the decrypted CKKS plaintext
polynomials and validated through Groth16 zero-knowledge proofs.

The actuation command includes:
\begin{itemize}
    \item a unique packet identifier,
    \item verified torque tensor values,
    \item bounded-error residual metadata,
    \item proof validation status,
    \item safety interlock flags.
\end{itemize}

Only commands whose associated proofs satisfy the bounded-error constraints
described in Chapter~4 are permitted to propagate to the hardware layer.

\section{Latency Constraints and Real-Time Guarantees}

The BAAC-SE v1.0 system enforces a strict end-to-end latency bound:
\[
\Delta t_{\text{total}} \le 12.5\ \mathrm{ms},
\]
which includes:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item interrupt ingestion on Core~0,
    \item CKKS evaluation on Core~1,
    \item Groth16 proof generation and verification on Core~2,
    \item actuation dispatch to hardware controllers.
\end{enumerate}

This latency constraint ensures that bionic devices respond to astrophysical
telemetry and user-driven control signals with naturalistic timing, preventing
oscillation, instability, or delayed motion.

\section{Cryptographic Safety Interlock}

The safety interlock is a hardware-level enforcement mechanism triggered by the
verification gatekeeper on Core~2. The interlock is activated under any of the
following conditions:
\begin{itemize}
    \item violation of the infinity norm bound:
    \[
    \| e_{\text{error}} \|_\infty > B,
    \]
    \item failure of Groth16 proof verification,
    \item malformed or incomplete witness data,
    \item corrupted telemetry or ciphertext states.
\end{itemize}

Upon activation, the interlock overrides all pending actuation commands and
forces the bionic system into a secure baseline posture:
\[
\bm{a}(t) = \bm{0},
\]
ensuring that no unsafe or unverified motion is executed.

\section{Hardware Integration and Control Path}

The actuation subsystem interfaces with:
\begin{itemize}
    \item servo-driven prosthetic joints,
    \item exoskeletal torque amplifiers,
    \item neural-interface microcontrollers,
    \item embedded motor-control ASICs.
\end{itemize}

Commands are transmitted over low-latency buses such as CAN-FD, EtherCAT, or
custom FPGA-backed control channels. The cryptographic verification flag is
embedded directly into the command packet, enabling hardware controllers to
reject any unverified or unsafe actuation signals.

\section{Interlock Response Time}

The interlock response time is bounded by:
\[
\Delta t_{\text{interlock}} \le 2\ \mathrm{ms},
\]
which ensures:
\begin{itemize}
    \item immediate cessation of unsafe motion,
    \item prevention of joint overextension,
    \item protection against corrupted astrophysical telemetry,
    \item compliance with biomechanical safety standards.
\end{itemize}

This rapid response is enabled by the core-isolated execution model described in
Chapter~6, which guarantees that the verification gatekeeper is never delayed by
background operating system tasks.

